Day 56: Cross-Site Scripting (XSS) — Testing & Remediation

Overview

Today I studied Cross-Site Scripting (XSS), focusing on how unsafe handling of user input can allow HTML or JavaScript to be interpreted by a browser. I created a local Python web application and implemented HTML output escaping to improve its security.

Lab Environment

- Platform: Kali Linux
- Language: Python 3
- Local endpoint: "http

Command:
"curl -i "http://127.0.0.1:8000/search?q=Hello""

Result: The application returned "200 OK" and reflected "Hello" in its HTML response.

2. Unsafe HTML Handling

Command:
"curl -i "http://127.0.0.1:8000/search?q=%3Cb%3EHello%3C%2Fb%3E""

In the initial vulnerable version, the response included "<b>Hello</b>" as HTML markup. This demonstrated that user input was being inserted into the HTML without output escaping.

3. Remediation and Verification

I implemented Python's "html.escape()" to encode special HTML characters before inserting the search term into the HTML page.

